==Welsh==

===Etymology===
May derive from {{com|cy|a|t1=''interrogative particle''|yw|t2=is, are}} or from or via the synonymous {{m|cy|ae}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|cy|/ai̯/}}
* {{rhymes|cy|ai̯|s=1}}

===Particle===
{{head|cy|particle}}

# {{non-gloss|interrogative particle}} {{qualifier|used when a non-verbal element is fronted for emphasis}}
#: {{syn|cy|ife}}
#: {{ux|cy|'''Ai''' ef yw dy dad?|t=Is he your father?}}
# [[whether]], [[if]] {{qualifier|used when the subordinate clause has a non-verbal element fronted for emphasis}}
#: {{ux|cy|Ni wn '''ai''' ef yw dy dad.|t=I do not know '''whether''' he is your father.}}

====Usage notes====
* Used before a non-verbal element fronted for emphasis. Unemphatic initial verbs, on the other hand, employ the interrogative particle {{m|cy|a}}.
* This word is found in formal language. As an initial interrogative particle, it is often dropped altogether in colloquial language or replaced with {{m|cy|ife}} in some southern dialects.
* Likewise, with the meaning "whether", this may be dropped colloquially. An alternative construction influenced by English is to replaced the {{m|cy|ai}} with {{m|cy|os||if}} followed by the appropriate dialect-specific indicating an emphatic subordinate clause, namely {{m|cy|taw}} in south Wales and {{m|cy|mai}} or {{m|cy|na}} in the north.

